<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Gjirokastër</h1><p class="st-meta">1386 CE – 1418 CE (approx.) · capital Gjirokastër</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Albanian lordship of the Zenebishi family in southern Albania, one of the last Christian powers in Epirus before the Ottoman conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-zenebishi">Gjon Zenebishi</a>, an Albanian chieftain whose clan had moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> during the 14th-century Albanian migrations, took the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a> (Argyrokastron) around 1386. He extended his rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drino">Drino</a> valley and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chameria">Chameria</a>, and the Byzantine court recognised him with the title of sebastokrator.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The lordship was a clan-based military domain built around the hilltop citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a> and its villages. Gjon fought the Italian rulers of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esau-de-buondelmonti">Esau de' Buondelmonti</a> in 1399, and courted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which controlled nearby <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corcyra">Corfu</a>. Society rested on Albanian pastoral clans and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Greek and Albanian peasantry.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Repeated Ottoman campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mehmed I wore the lordship down, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a> fell in 1418, the year Gjon died. His son Depa fled to Venetian territory and later led a failed rising in 1434. The town became the centre of an Ottoman sanjak.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1386 CE – 1418 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Balkans and Hungary</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.28%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1386 CE – 1418 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gjirokastër</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gjon Zenebishi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Albania, Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zenebishi principality, Lordship of Argyrokastron</dd></dl></aside></div>

